---
name: scaffold
description: One-time project scaffold on the locked stack (framework Part 6). Bootstrap only; run once, then never again.
disable-model-invocation: true
---

Scaffold {{PROJECT_NAME}} on the locked stack ({{STACK}}). This runs exactly
once, after phase 2 of the bootstrap; a repo that is already scaffolded gets
/setlist:validate instead.

1. Scaffold the project on the stack recorded in steering/tech.md. Create the
   pure-logic skeleton under {{SRC_ROLE}}/ and the test harness under
   {{TESTS_ROLE}}/, keeping the boundary steering/structure.md draws between
   pure logic and framework/UI code.

   [PHASE 2 SLOT: the exact scaffold commands for this stack (init, dependency
   install, test runner, linter, typechecker, CI workflow), filled during
   tailored generation from the Step 2 decisions.]

2. Wire the gates: tests, lint, typecheck, build. Record the single command that
   runs the FULL suite in .claude/sdd.json as "gate_command" (the close gate and
   /setlist:checkpoint run it verbatim).

   **The command must run in a BARE shell, with no environment activated
   first.** The close gate and the git hooks run it in their own subshell, which
   inherits whatever git or the harness happened to have on PATH, not your
   interactive shell. So a project whose toolchain lives in a virtualenv, an nvm
   or rbenv shim, or a Conda env records the activation as part of the command
   (`. .venv/bin/activate && ...`, `poetry run ...`, `npm exec -- ...`), or
   calls the tools by their in-project paths. VERIFY it before recording it:

       ( eval "$(jq -r .gate_command .claude/sdd.json)" ) >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?

   A non-zero here means the gate is not runnable by the hooks even when the
   suite itself is green. The refusal that follows is indistinguishable from a
   real failure, and the operator who knows their suite passes is then one step
   from disabling the boundary to get past it. Get this right at scaffold time
   and that never comes up.

3. Flip "scaffolded" to true in .claude/sdd.json. This arms the scope hook:
   from the next write on, feature code on the trunk branch (the "trunk"
   field in .claude/sdd.json) is blocked, so finish the scaffold before
   flipping.

4. Write the scaffold record to journal/0001-scaffold.md: dependency versions,
   files created, anything machine-specific learned during the install. Point
   to it from specs/STATUS.md (Pointers), and name the next action in Current
   state.

5. Commit the scaffold as one commit on the trunk (the one-time exception to
   the branch rule; the scope hook is armed only after step 3, which is why
   steps run in this order). If no git repo exists yet, run git init first
   with the trunk named in .claude/sdd.json as the initial branch
   (git init -b <trunk>) and make this the repo's first commit, covering the
   uncommitted session-zero output. Do not push.

   ARM THE GIT-HOOK BOUNDARY IMMEDIATELY AFTER git init, BEFORE that first
   commit, and verify it took:

       git config core.hooksPath .githooks
       git config merge.ff false
       git config --get core.hooksPath    # must print .githooks

   This is not optional and it is not cosmetic. /setlist:new runs in an EMPTY
   directory, so stamp.sh could not set these: there was no repository yet, and
   it says so in its own output. Until they are set the hooks sit in .githooks/
   and git never runs them, which means the project has NO enforcement at all
   while appearing fully set up. Measured before this step existed: a secret
   committed, an unclosed spec merged to the trunk, and both reached a remote,
   every command exiting 0 with no hook output (v1.7 claims audit, R3-1).

   If .githooks/ is absent, stop and say so rather than continuing: the
   instance was not stamped with the git-hook boundary and /scaffold cannot
   invent it.
